## Step-by-step: build and test in Antigravity

Work in 6 phases. Each phase ends with your review, so a mistake never spreads.

**Setup (once)**

1. Back up first. Run `git init` (if not already a repo), `git add -A`, `git commit -m "baseline before Partner Hub"`, then `git checkout -b feature/partner-hub`. Also take a `pg_dump` of the database.
2. Export this doc as Markdown and save it as `VivasAI/docs/PIH_SRS.md`.
3. Open the `VivasAI` folder in Antigravity. Set the agent to **Planning** mode and terminal and review policy to **Request review**, so nothing runs or changes without your OK.
4. Paste the **Master prompt**. Check the Implementation Plan artifact it returns: it must list reused tables and APIs, and say “no existing files modified”. Comment on the plan in the artifact if anything is off, then approve.

**Phase prompts (paste one at a time, after the previous phase passes)**

| Phase | Prompt to paste | You test |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1 Data | “Phase 1: create `database/partner_hub_migration.sql` and its rollback, per SRS Data model. Run them on a copy DB first and show me `\dt pih_*`.” | Migration runs twice without error; rollback removes only `pih_` tables; existing tables unchanged |
| 2 Partners + catalogue | “Phase 2: build `routes/partnerHub.js` partners, documents, stores, products, SKUs, composition, suitability, claims, import, plus the pages PartnerProducts, PartnerAdmin (verification, claims, approval tabs). Show me the A1/A2 diff separately.” | Register a partner, upload an FCO licence, SME approves, add product, invalid GSTIN rejected, product without licence blocked |
| 3 Store + checkout | “Phase 3: Marketplace, StorePage, ProductDetail, CartCheckout, MyOrders, PartnerOrders. Checkout must create `vivas_purchase_orders` and the vendor through existing APIs; receive must create `vivas_inputs_lots`.” | Buy a product; PO appears in Buy & Procurement; vendor appears in Contacts; after Receive, lot appears in Assets › Materials & Inputs |
| 4 Evidence + AI + rating | “Phase 4: sync worker, `pih_treatment_outcomes`, ProductResults, `services/pihAiEngine.js`, FPS rating job, FieldInsights, FieldTrials. Seed 200 synthetic outcomes.” | Log a treatment in Crop Health → outcome row appears; FPS changes after a verified outcome; under 10 outcomes shows “Needs Data”; partner never sees farm names or GPS |
| 5 Recommendations | “Phase 5: recommendation generation on case, alert and soil-test triggers; Recommendations page with Buy / Add to list / Create task / Dismiss.” | Confirm a diagnosis → up to 3 suitable products appear with reasons; PHI conflict is suppressed; Create task shows in Tasks |
| 6 Integrations | “Phase 6: `routes/partnerApiV1.js`, API keys, webhooks with HMAC and retries, OpenAPI at /api/pih/v1/docs, Integrations page, consent page, audit log.” | Call the API with a key (Postman or curl); revoked key gets 401; webhook test delivers and verifies signature; consent withdrawal drops that farm from aggregates |

**After each phase**

1. Ask Antigravity: “Run the app, open each new page in the browser, and give me a walkthrough artifact with screenshots.” Review the screenshots against the Vivas AI look: same sidebar, cards and colours.
2. Run a regression check yourself: open Dashboard, Tasks, Crop Health, Buy & Procurement, Assets and Settings, and confirm they behave as before.
3. Commit with a phase message, e.g. `git commit -m "PIH phase 2: partners and catalogue"`.
4. Approve the A1/A2 wiring diff only when phase 2 passes. Leave the B items for a later, separate request.

**Go-live**

1. Run the migration on production during a quiet window, after a fresh `pg_dump`.
2. Deploy with `./deploy.sh`, then start the PM2 worker (A3).
3. Onboard 2–3 pilot partners and 10 pilot farms with consent. Watch `pih_audit_log`, `pih_jobs` errors and webhook failures for 2 weeks before opening the Hub to everyone.
